{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | sed -n '78,245p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | sed -n '302,357p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | sed -n '420,690p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | sed -n '690,820p'\"","aggregated_output":"    78\ttranslate between kernels. The foreign-engine case (an engine that\n    79\timplements canonical semantic-VM execution in its own layout, UCF §7.4.2)\n    80\tis the semantic profile lowered through that engine's value profile, and\n    81\tvalue profiles are a separate document (§10.6).\n    82\t\n    83\t## 3. Rows, id spaces, and the census\n    84\t\n    85\tEvery row is `[id kind & slots]`; the stored payload is the body\n    86\t`[kind & slots]`; the id is an address envelope outside the hashed bytes,\n    87\tas for code rows (code-as-tuples §2.1).\n    88\t\n    89\t**The wire unit** is one closed map, so that the gate of §8 has\n    90\tsomething to run on before any row is interpreted:\n    91\t\n    92\t```clojure\n    93\t{:yin.k/handoff true\n    94\t :yin.k/version 3\n    95\t :yin.k/root    B          ; the root :task row's id\n    96\t :yin.k/rows    [[id kind & slots] …]}   ; every row of the tree, any order\n    97\t```\n    98\t\n    99\tUnlisted keys refuse. Duplicate ids among `:yin.k/rows` refuse before\n   100\tany traversal (`:yin.k/undecodable`), so every walk is over a map from\n   101\tid to one row. The gate's walk over install children follows only\n   102\t`:install` child crefs and keeps an **active recursion-path set**: a\n   103\tcref that leaves the rows, or a child that is already on the active\n   104\tpath, stops the gate with `:yin.k/undecodable`. A child reached again\n   105\t*off* the active path is not an error: two install entries whose\n   106\tchildren have identical content (two identical halted children, say)\n   107\tshare one child row by Merkle construction, the structural graph is a\n   108\tDAG, and each install entry still restores as its own native task\n   109\tinstance with its own task-local cells and its own contextual custody\n   110\tvalidation. Completed content validation is cached per row; instance\n   111\trestoration never is. When a pack is addressed (§10.2) this map is what\n   112\tis hashed, and a `:yin.k/root` that does not name a `:task` row in\n   113\t`:rows` is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n   114\t\n   115\t**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body {:algorithm\n   116\t:sha256})`: this carrier pins SHA-256 explicitly, because\n   117\t`segment-key`'s one-argument default is Jing's own default algorithm\n   118\t(BLAKE3 at the time of writing) and a canonical address scheme must be\n   119\tunique to be canonical. An id carrying any other multihash is\n   120\t`:yin.k/undecodable`. Code addresses `A` are not touched by this pin:\n   121\tthey keep their governing address function (`ucf/code-address`), and\n   122\tthe carrier verifies them under it. The digest choice is a proposal\n   123\t(§10). Equal bodies are one row. The id is Merkle: it commits to every\n   124\trow id in the body.\n   125\t\n   126\t**Identity rows** are cells (UCF §7.5.3). A cell has a task-local id `C`\n   127\tin the form `:yin.k/c-<n>`, minted by the lift under §6's canonical\n   128\tnumbering, never content-addressed, and scoped to the task whose root\n   129\tcarries it: a cell id means nothing outside its root, in a pool or a\n   130\tquery, and two tasks' `:yin.k/c-1` are unrelated.\n   131\t\n   132\t**The census.** A cell's *contents* (its stream marker and kept position)\n   133\tare not reachable through its id, so a content row that references a\n   134\tcell commits to the cell's identity but not to what the cell holds. The\n   135\troot therefore carries a **cell census**, a content row\n   136\t\n   137\t```clojure\n   138\t[X :cells  C₁ B₁  C₂ B₂  …]          ; sorted by n; Bᵢ is a cref\n   139\t[Bᵢ :cell-body  stream-cref  position]\n   140\t```\n   141\t\n   142\tthat maps every cell id to the content row of its body. The census is a\n   143\tslot of the root, so the root id commits to every cell's contents. A\n   144\tchange to any cell's position or stream changes `Bᵢ`, the census, and\n   145\tthe root. Identity references preserve aliasing; the census\n   146\tauthenticates contents. The validation rules (§8) require the census to\n   147\tlist exactly the cells the task reaches: none missing, none extra, per\n   148\troot and per install child.\n   149\t\n   150\t**Acyclicity.** Two edge families exist and are checked separately:\n   151\t\n   152\t- *Structural value edges*: a cref in a slot, a cell iref in a value, a\n   153\t  census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n   154\t  and a `:cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n   155\t  (data), so no cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n   156\t  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 3), because a receiver\n   157\t  validates, it does not trust construction.\n   158\t- *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n   159\t  resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n   160\t  a manifest address `M` resolved through the module stores; an\n   161\t  `:install` pending names a module whose entry holds a child body.\n   162\t  These may close cycles and that is legal: a module store that holds a\n   163\t  closure whose `store-of` is that same module is the ordinary shape of\n   164\t  a linked module, and self- or mutually-referencing parked records\n   165\t  resolve through the explicit `P` table exactly as amendment §6 states.\n   166\t  A guest value that would need a cycle through *structural* edges is\n   167\t  refused at lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:cyclic` naming the\n   168\t  path, unchanged from UCF §7.5.3.\n   169\t\n   170\t## 4. The value union\n   171\t\n   172\tEvery position that holds a guest value holds one **encoded value** `V`,\n   173\ta two-element vector whose first element is a fixed tag:\n   174\t\n   175\t| `V` | Meaning |\n   176\t|---|---|\n   177\t| `[:lit d]` | a literal: `d` is in the portable literal domain below, carried by Jing as data |\n   178\t| `[:row B]` | a reference to the content row `B` (a closure, a collection row, a frame value, a primitive, a stream marker) |\n   179\t| `[:cell C]` | a reference to the identity cell `C` |\n   180\t\n   181\tThe union is disjoint by the tag, not by the shape or spelling of `d`: a\n   182\tliteral keyword that happens to equal a row address is `[:lit k]` and\n   183\tcan never be read as a reference; a literal vector that looks like a\n   184\t`[:row …]` pair is `[:lit [...]]`. No decision depends on content, which\n   185\tis the UCF §7.5.1 property restated for rows.\n   186\t\n   187\t**The portable literal domain** is UCF §7.5.1's scalar and collection\n   188\tarms, closed under nesting, with exactly the numeric classes Jing and\n   189\t`value-encoder` already admit: nil, booleans, exact integers of any width\n   190\t(Jing major types 0/1, tags 2/3), float64 carriers, Jing's Decimal and\n   191\tRational carriers under Jing's normalization rules, strings, keywords,\n   192\tsymbols, and vectors, lists, sets and maps *all of whose elements, keys,\n   193\tvalues and retained metadata are in the domain*. Carrier classes are\n   194\tretained, never coerced (an integral float stays a float). Byte strings\n   195\tare not admitted by this carrier and refuse as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n   196\tkind `:byte-string`.\n   197\t\n   198\t**Metadata** is walked like any other part of a value when deciding both\n   199\tportability and whether a value reaches a reference. Reference-free\n   200\tretained metadata on a *literal* travels as Jing preserves it and is\n   201\thashed as Jing hashes it. Collection rows have no metadata slot, so\n   202\t**any collection that must become a row and carries retained metadata\n   203\trefuses**, whether the metadata holds a reference (copying it as data\n   204\twould carry source coordinates and seals unremapped) or is ordinary\n   205\treference-free data such as `{:doc \"x\"}`: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n   206\t`:metadata` naming the path. This is a proposed limitation of this\n   207\tcarrier, not an inherited UCF requirement (§10). Metadata a reader strips\n   208\tis not retained metadata and plays no part.\n   209\t\n   210\tA guest map is always wrapped, `[:lit m]` or a `:map` row, and is open\n   211\tdata inside the wrapper; structural rows are closed.\n   212\t\n   213\tA collection whose elements, keys, values or metadata include a\n   214\treference is not a literal; it becomes a row (§5.1) and the position\n   215\tholds `[:row B]`. A collection that reaches no reference is a literal\n   216\tand **must** be `[:lit d]`: a reference-free `:vec`, `:list`, `:set` or\n   217\t`:map` row is a non-canonical spelling and refuses (§8). Empty\n   218\tcollections are therefore always literals. This is the only structural\n   219\tdecision the encoder makes and it is decided by one question, *does this\n   220\tcollection reach a reference?*, answered by walking it.\n   221\t\n   222\t**Structural data** is a second, separate domain: values that UCF\n   223\t§7.4.3 requires to be in the canonical-bytes domain but that are not\n   224\tguest literals and contain no guest values, namely install responses,\n   225\tlink envelopes, operation ids, link ids and call ids. A structural-data\n   226\tslot holds plain canonical-bytes data and is validated by the rule that\n   227\tgoverns it (an install response by the linker's delivery checks, an\n   228\top-id by §7.4.3 r5), not by the literal domain. Table rows mark these\n   229\tslots `D`. A retained **FFI request envelope is not structural data**:\n   230\t`dao.stream.apply/request?` permits unknown envelope keys, and its\n   231\targuments are guest values. It therefore travels as the existing\n   232\tencoder carries it (`handoff.cljc`): the **complete envelope,\n   233\trecursively encoded as a `V`** (a `:map` row, or `[:lit m]` when it\n   234\treaches no reference), so that every key is preserved, closures stay\n   235\tportable, and resource references go through the census. No closed\n   236\tenvelope row narrows the open request protocol.\n   237\t\n   238\tPositions that hold structural data (addresses, pcs, symbols as binding\n   239\tnames, reasons, phases, ids) are typed by the row table and are not `V`;\n   240\ttheir kinds are fixed per slot, as for code rows.\n   241\t\n   242\t## 5. The row table\n   243\t\n   244\tKinds are closed. An unknown kind, a wrong arity, a slot whose content is\n   245\tnot of the tabled kind, or a `V` outside §4's union is `:yin.k/undecodable`\n   302\tsegment pc env stack-base}`; the semantic profile has no other frame\n   303\ttype on the wire (walker effect frames belong to the walker profile's\n   304\town table). Resume pc and return pc must lie inside their segment, the\n   305\treturn pc immediately after a non-tail call, stack bases nondecreasing\n   306\tfrom outermost to innermost and each at most the enclosing stack length\n   307\t(amendment §5.1), all checked in §8.\n   308\t\n   309\t### 5.3 Pending (content rows)\n   310\t\n   311\tOne kind, `:pending`, whose first slot is the reason and whose remaining\n   312\tslots are fixed per reason. The reasons are UCF §7.4.3's closed union as\n   313\tthe amendment r5 required-keys list fixes them; `:park` and\n   314\t`:call-effect` are not reasons and refuse as `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n   315\t\n   316\t| reason | slots after the reason |\n   317\t|---|---|\n   318\t| `:next` | `cell-C` |\n   319\t| `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id-D` |\n   320\t| `:ffi` | `cell-C  call-id-D  op` (op may be nil) |\n   321\t| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  envelope-V  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n   322\t| `:link-request` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C  envelope-D  op-id-D` |\n   323\t| `:link-response` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C` |\n   324\t| `:install` | `name-S` |\n   325\t\n   326\tThe retained FFI request's `envelope-V` is the complete envelope map,\n   327\trecursively encoded under §4's ordinary rules: `[:lit m]` when it\n   328\treaches no reference (the common case, arguments such as `[1 2]`), a\n   329\t`:map` row otherwise. Lower decodes it and retries it verbatim; it\n   330\tnever recomputes the arguments. Correlation must agree, not merely be present:\n   331\ta cell's `:cell-body` stream is the pending's response stream; an\n   332\tenvelope's link id or call id equals the pending's; a `:link-request`\n   333\tenvelope names the pending's module. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\n   334\tpresent exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\n   335\twriter, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\n   336\tand subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n   337\t`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, unique across the task and its children, absent\n   338\twhen the body has no origin). `request-envelope` and `envelope` are the\n   339\tretained requests, retried verbatim. `call-id` and `link-id` are plain.\n   340\tAn `:install` pending is never sufficient alone: the root's installs\n   341\tslot must hold the entry for `name`, else lift refuses\n   342\t`:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:incomplete-install` and a body carrying it\n   343\tis `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n   344\t\n   345\t### 5.4 Task context (content rows)\n   346\t\n   347\t| Kind | Slots |\n   348\t|---|---|\n   349\t| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by key; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n   350\t| `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n   351\t| `:parked-table` | `P₁ regs-cref₁ P₂ regs-cref₂ …` sorted by P |\n   352\t| `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent-D  response-D  child-cref` |\n   353\t| `:cells` | the census, §3 |\n   354\t| `:cell-body` | `stream-cref  position` (position in the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` portable cursor domain) |\n   355\t\n   356\tResource entries never appear in `:store`; streams and cells lower into\n   357\tthe receiver's private resource table beside the program store (linker\n   420\tare inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\n   421\tplain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\n   422\tkeeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\n   423\tthe carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n   424\t\n   425\t## 6. Canonical cell numbering\n   426\t\n   427\tCell ids must be a function of the state, or two lifts of one state give\n   428\ttwo root ids. Local rules are not enough: a cell's number must depend on\n   429\teverything that distinguishes it, including references to it from\n   430\telsewhere in the task (the review's counterexample: `'a` bound to a set\n   431\tholding cells X and Y with equal bodies, `'b` bound to X; any rule that\n   432\tlooks only at the set and the bodies cannot tell X from Y, but `'b`\n   433\tcan). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\n   434\ttopology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\n   435\twhich any algorithm is checked.\n   436\t\n   437\t**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering\n   438\tis defined as **the output of the procedure below**, which is a function\n   439\tof the state alone: every step depends only on structure, never on a\n   440\tcell's number or on any hash that includes one, so renaming the cells of\n   441\tthe input does not change the output. It is *not* claimed to be the\n   442\tminimum of the root bytes over all `n!` bijections; that is a different\n   443\tdefinition, and a refinement-and-search procedure is not shown to reach\n   444\tit (signature order does not order Merkle bytes). Nor is it claimed that\n   445\tcells left together by refinement are interchangeable: they need not be\n   446\t(a guest set encoding a disjoint triangle and square as ordered pairs of\n   447\tequal-body cells leaves all seven cells in one class, and no\n   448\tautomorphism exchanges a triangle vertex with a square vertex). The\n   449\tprocedure therefore explores every unresolved alternative, with no\n   450\tpruning, and the representative it returns is canonical because the\n   451\tprocedure is deterministic and renaming-invariant, not because of any\n   452\tsymmetry argument.\n   453\t\n   454\t**The procedure.** Its input is the abstract task: the structure of\n   455\t§5 with cells as identities, before any numbering. Every encoding below\n   456\tis canonical CBOR (`dao.jing.cbor.md`) of a *synthetic vector\n   457\tstructure*; vectors are used throughout, never host maps or sets, so\n   458\tthat elements or keys that become equal after class substitution keep\n   459\ttheir multiplicity. All byte comparisons are bytewise on those CBOR\n   460\tbytes. Let the cells be `n` in number; if `n = 0` the procedure returns\n   461\tthe empty numbering and performs no search.\n   462\t\n   463\t1. **Class labels** are byte strings. A cell's initial label is the\n   464\t   CBOR of `[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`.\n   465\t2. **Class encoding** `CE(x)` of any encoded value or row, structural\n   466\t   and hash-free:\n   467\t   - `[:lit d]` → `[:lit d]`.\n   468\t   - `[:cell C]` → `[:cell <label of C>]` (full tag retained, §10.9).\n   469\t   - `[:row B]` → `[:row <CE(row B)>]`, the row expanded in place; the\n   470\t     expansion is finite because the structural edge family is acyclic\n   471\t     (§8 step 3).\n   472\t   - A row `[kind s₁ … sₖ]` → `[kind CE(s₁) … CE(sₖ)]` with each slot\n   473\t     encoded as: a structural datum (`A`, `M`, `P`, `S`, `N`, `D`, a\n   474\t     reason, a phase) as itself; a `V` as above; a cref as the expanded\n   475\t     row; a crefs vector as the vector of expansions.\n   476\t   - For `:set` rows, and for the key-value pairs of `:map`, `:store`\n   477\t     and `:bindings` rows and the entries of `:parked-table`, `:cells`\n   478\t     and the installs and module-stores slots: encode each element (or\n   479\t     each `[key value]` pair as a two-element vector), then **sort the\n   480\t     resulting vector of encodings bytewise, retaining duplicates**.\n   481\t     Ordered rows (`:vec`, `:list`, `:stack`, `:k`, frames) keep their\n   482\t     order.\n   483\t   - Context lookups (`P` in `:parked-ref` and `parked-id`, `M` in\n   484\t     `store-of`) are not expanded; they appear as the plain datum.\n   485\t   - An install child's `:task` row is **not expanded**: children are\n   486\t     numbered first, as their own tasks with their own cells (task\n   487\t     boundary), and a child cref appears in the parent's encodings as\n   488\t     `[:child <child root id>]`, the child's already-canonical root id.\n   489\t3. **Positions.** A position is the CBOR of the path from the task's\n   490\t   root to a cell reference, a vector of steps, each step one of\n   491\t   `[kind slot-index]` for a tabled slot, `[kind :elem CE(e)]` for an\n   492\t   element of a sorted row, `[kind :key CE(k)]` and `[kind :val CE(k)]`\n   493\t   for the key and value sides of a keyed row's pair, and `[:frame i]`\n   494\t   for the `i`th wait. Where several equal elements exist, equal\n   495\t   positions are repeated, one per occurrence.\n   496\t4. **Signature and refinement.** A cell's signature is the CBOR of\n   497\t   `[:sig <old label> <sorted vector of its positions, duplicates\n   498\t   retained>]`; its new label is that byte string. Repeat until no\n   499\t   class splits. Keeping the old label makes refinement monotone, so\n   500\t   the partition stabilizes in at most `n − k` splitting rounds from\n   501\t   `k` initial classes plus one stability check; this bounds partition\n   502\t   rounds only, not signature-processing or search work.\n   503\t5. **Search.** Order classes bytewise by label. If every class is a\n   504\t   singleton, assign `1 … n` in that order and stop. Otherwise take the\n   505\t   first non-singleton class and, **for each of its members in turn**,\n   506\t   give that member the label `[:ind <search depth> <old label>]`\n   507\t   (distinct from every refinement label by its tag; no ordering\n   508\t   relative to other labels is needed), refine to stability, and\n   509\t   recurse. Every branch is explored. Each complete leaf yields a\n   510\t   numbering; build the §5 rows under it and take the **root body's\n   511\t   canonical CBOR bytes** (the digest preimage, not the digest) as the\n   512\t   leaf's key. The procedure's output is the row set of the leaf with\n   513\t   the bytewise least key. Two leaves with equal keys have emitted\n   514\t   identical rows: they differ only in which source cell received\n   515\t   which number, which the transported representation cannot observe.\n   516\t\n   517\t**Validation.** Canonicality is a property of the emitted rows, not of\n   518\ta source-cell-to-number bijection, so the receiver does not compare\n   519\tnumberings. It decodes the rows to the abstract task, runs the\n   520\tprocedure, re-emits rows, and compares the resulting root id with the\n   521\tcarried root id: unequal is `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n   522\t`:noncanonical-cells`. Every labeling that yields the selected\n   523\tcommitment is thereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever\n   524\tdecides validity. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\n   525\tkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\n   526\tprocedure completes refuses with a structured resource refusal before\n   527\tany attachment and never labels the state `:noncanonical-cells`, never\n   528\tfalls back to encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check.\n   529\t\n   530\tUnder this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\n   531\tdifferent states sharing a closure over the same cell may number it\n   532\tdifferently and so not share that closure's row across lifts; that\n   533\tcross-lift dedup is knowingly given up for identity-bearing values and\n   534\tnever affected rows below a cell. On lower, each `C` becomes one fresh\n   535\tprivate resource entry seeded at its carried position; every `[:cell C]`\n   536\tmaps to that entry; two references to one cell share it and one\n   537\treference per cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n   538\t\n   539\t## 7. Equality and the content address\n   540\t\n   541\tThe transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\n   542\tthe root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cell\n   543\tid through the references, and to every cell's contents through the\n   544\tcensus. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,\n   545\tand under §6 that holds iff they were lifted from states equal up to the\n   546\trenaming of cell ids that §6 makes canonical. No physical enumeration of\n   547\tthe row set needs an order, because the root commits to everything and\n   548\tthe pack, if one is addressed, has its own canonical encoding (§10.2).\n   549\tJing supplies canonical bytes and the digest of each body; this document\n   550\tsupplies the order within each body and the numbering of cells. Jing is\n   551\tasked to sort nothing it does not already sort.\n   552\t\n   553\tOccurrence and origin are inside the hash, as in versions 1 and 2 where\n   554\tthe body address is the hash of the whole body. Snapshot variants of one\n   555\toccurrence may therefore have distinct addresses while remaining one\n   556\toccurrence, exactly as UCF §7.3.4 intends.\n   557\t\n   558\t## 8. Validation on lower\n   559\t\n   560\tValidation is pure until the last step and checks the whole tree before\n   561\tone attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n   562\t\n   563\t1. **Gate.** Decode the wire map's canonical bytes retaining numeric\n   564\t   classes; the tag is present and the outer version is the exact\n   565\t   integer 3, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (version found, supported\n   566\t   set). Reject duplicate row ids and a `:yin.k/root` that names no\n   567\t   `:task` row (`:yin.k/undecodable`). Walk install children from the\n   568\t   root in canonical module-name order with an **active recursion-path\n   569\t   set** (§3), following only `:install` child crefs; a cref outside the\n   570\t   rows, a child already on the active path, or a non-`:task` child\n   571\t   stops the gate as `:yin.k/undecodable`; a child reached again off\n   572\t   the path is gated once and its result cached. Gate every\n   573\t   child's version, then every profile map against the registry (only\n   574\t   the semantic profile is supported, §2), then require all versions 3\n   575\t   and all profiles equal to the root's. Malformed task structure is\n   576\t   `:yin.k/undecodable`; an unsupported version or profile is\n   577\t   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; the two are never conflated.\n   578\t2. **Structure, non-recursive.** Every row's kind is tabled with the\n   579\t   right arity; every structural slot holds its tabled kind by local\n   580\t   inspection; every `V` has one of the three tags; every `D` is in the\n   581\t   canonical bytes domain; exactly one row has role `:root`; kind rules\n   582\t   of §5.5 hold; the custody slot matches the role, kind and policy\n   583\t   table. Nothing in this step follows a reference.\n   584\t3. **Graph.** Every cref and every `[:row B]` names a present row of a\n   585\t   kind admissible in that position; the structural edge family is\n   586\t   acyclic (checked with an active-path walk, bounded by the row count);\n   587\t   every row is reachable from the root through structural edges (an\n   588\t   unreachable row is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every `[:cell C]` names a\n   589\t   census entry and every census entry is reached **through the task's\n   590\t   own roots, the census's declaration edges excluded**, per task and\n   591\t   per child; every `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves in the parked\n   592\t   table; every `store-of` resolves to a carried module store; every\n   593\t   `:install` pending has its entry and every entry's child is a\n   594\t   `:child` task. After this step every traversal below is finite and\n   595\t   safe.\n   596\t4. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals the SHA-256 address of its\n   597\t   body; every code payload hashes to its key `A` and is well formed\n   598\t   under the stamp; the requested body address, if one was given, equals\n   599\t   the root id. Mismatch is `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or\n   600\t   code key.\n   601\t5. **Canonical form, recursive.** Every literal is in the portable\n   602\t   domain; every sorted slot is in the §5 order; every keyed row has\n   603\t   unique keys, and every `:set` row unique elements, under canonical\n   604\t   bytes and under portable decoded equality; optional absence has its\n   605\t   one spelling (nil in the tabled slot); a reference-free collection is\n   606\t   spelled `[:lit d]`, never a row; empty collections are literals;\n   607\t   metadata obeys §4. A breach is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row\n   608\t   and slot. Without this step two different valid encodings could lower\n   609\t   to one state, or a `:bindings` row could carry one symbol twice and\n   610\t   lose a binding on lower.\n   611\t6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 under the work bound and\n   612\t   compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cells`; or the\n   613\t   resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound is exceeded).\n   614\t7. **Code and frames.** Each wait's resume pc is a static safepoint\n   615\t   whose kinds admit the wait's reason (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n   616\t   `:reason-mismatch`); a parked record's pc is an explicit-park\n   617\t   safepoint; a reified value's pc is a `:current-continuation`\n   618\t   successor; return pcs follow a non-tail call; stack bases are bounded\n   619\t   and nondecreasing; closure params agree with the addressed lambda;\n   620\t   integer slots are in range.\n   621\t8. **Pending and operations.** Reason-specific slot rules; correlation\n   622\t   agreement of §5.3 (cell stream, envelope ids, module names); every\n   623\t   op-id below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without\n   624\t   origin; every install response verified as §5.4 requires.\n   625\t9. **Dependency closure.** Compute the census of amendment §6 from the\n   626\t   rows (waits, pendings and envelopes, parked records, store, module\n   627\t   stores, result, children) and require the carried `requires` and\n   628\t   `code` map to agree with it exactly: a declared segment not reached\n   629\t   or a reached segment not declared is `:yin.k/undecodable`. Then\n   630\t   evaluate `requires` per UCF §7.6.5: missing segments, primitives,\n   631\t   modules, streams, cursor profiles are `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a body\n   632\t   with cells claims `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; module-store coverage is\n   633\t   complete for every `store-of` reached.\n   634\t10. **Custody.** For an exclusive root, the occurrence, baseline and\n   635\t    operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n   636\t    never adds missing header keys.\n   637\t11. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\n   638\t    the receiving composition.\n   639\t12. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n   640\t    isolated store, module-store instances (first link wins), one\n   641\t    private resource entry per cell, the parked table, children first\n   642\t    (one native task instance **per install entry**, even where two\n   643\t    entries share a child row), then waits in order; adopt `max(local,\n   644\t    carried)` for the id counter; publish the machine only after every\n   645\t    child succeeds; run, poll, mint, append, close or initialize nothing\n   646\t    during lower.\n   647\t\n   648\tDiagnostics use the established families and no new top-level outcome:\n   649\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\n   650\t`[row-id slot-index …]` and a `:yin.k/kind` where one is named above\n   651\t(`:reason-mismatch`, `:noncanonical-cells`), `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`,\n   652\t`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, and on the lift side `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n   653\tthe two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\n   654\t`:metadata`.\n   655\t\n   656\t## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n   657\t\n   658\t**Lift** (reference machine): refuse unless quiescent; number cells (§6);\n   659\twalk waits, parked records, the slice, module stores, the result and\n   660\teach install child, emitting rows and the census; refuse by path as UCF\n   661\t§7.5.4 and §7.2.1 specify. The nested carrier's value table\n   662\t(`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cell\n   663\ttable becomes the census.\n   664\t\n   665\t**Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n   666\t\n   667\tThe laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n   668\t\n   669\t- **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n   670\t  version-3 state `S` in the semantic profile **for which `lower(S)`\n   671\t  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n   672\t  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n   673\t  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n   674\t  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n   675\t  `max(local, carried)` is the carried value; occurrence, origin and the\n   676\t  custody arm are supplied to the lift from the restored task's own\n   677\t  record, not minted; cell bodies and op-ids are carried verbatim; the\n   678\t  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\n   679\t  (a receiver with a larger counter, or any step taken) the law does not\n   680\t  apply and native equivalence is the claim.\n   681\t- **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n   682\t  `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n   683\t  external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n   684\t  set and its order, the result, and the effect trace.\n   685\t- **Cross-engine.** Running the corpus to each safepoint on an engine\n   686\t  that implements canonical semantic-VM execution yields, under\n   687\t  controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n   688\t  descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n   689\t  reference machine yields.\n   690\t- **Legacy correspondence.** Versions 0, 1 and 2 are lowered directly by\n   690\t- **Legacy correspondence.** Versions 0, 1 and 2 are lowered directly by\n   691\t  their own version-aware readers, keeping their addresses and custody\n   692\t  bindings (§10.8). Normalization into version 3 is a separate tool and\n   693\t  is **partial**: a legacy body normalizes with semantic preservation\n   694\t  (same lowered machine under native equivalence) or refuses with one\n   695\t  of this carrier's named refusals (`:byte-string`, `:metadata`, a\n   696\t  profile version 3 does not yet support); it never substitutes a\n   697\t  version-3 address for an admitted legacy checkpoint. Exact mutual\n   698\t  inversion is required only between version 3 and its one canonical\n   699\t  nested projection (§10.3).\n   700\t\n   701\tThe UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. During transition the\n   702\tStage D/E corpus, re-encoded through the canonical projection, is the\n   703\toracle for the first law.\n   704\t\n   705\t## 10. Questions the review answered, and what remains\n   706\t\n   707\tAnswers 1–6 are the codex Architect's (round 1); where the Architect\n   708\tmarked a point as an owner decision it is labelled so.\n   709\t\n   710\t1. **Occurrence inside the hash.** Kept inside, following the governing\n   711\t   rule and binding custody context to the published checkpoint. An\n   712\t   occurrence-independent execution-state address is an owner-level\n   713\t   protocol decision; if ever pursued it must sit beside, not replace, a\n   714\t   fully authenticated checkpoint address, and it does not substitute\n   715\t   for the census.\n   716\t2. **Packing.** Individual immutable content rows plus a self-contained\n   717\t   transport pack. When a pack is addressed it gets its own address and\n   718\t   canonical encoding, distinct from the semantic root id; cell ids\n   719\t   remain root-scoped inside it. Same answer as code-as-tuples §2.1\n   720\t   should receive; the loader interface is resolved explicitly, not by\n   721\t   layout.\n   722\t3. **The nested grammar.** Versions 0, 1 and 2 remain compatibility\n   723\t   inputs. One canonical nested projection of version 3 is kept for\n   724\t   single-message channels and must stay exactly mutually inverse with\n   725\t   the rows. Rows are the carrier authority once specified. Retiring old\n   726\t   readers is an owner version-support decision.\n   727\t4. **Contract stamp.** The profile map and code contracts do not move; a\n   728\t   carrier change is a new body version with its own gate. Any change to\n   729\t   instruction or scheduler semantics would separately revise the\n   730\t   execution contract and is not proposed here.\n   731\t5. **Reified and parked.** Sharing immutable `:regs` rows is acceptable;\n   732\t   the two keep distinct outer kinds (§5.1) and distinct lowering. A\n   733\t   reified capture has no pending; a parked reference resolves in task\n   734\t   context.\n   735\t6. **Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before any implementation\n   736\t   brief claims heterogeneous execution conformance, and not drafted\n   737\t   here: admitted values, numeric behaviour, key equality, metadata,\n   738\t   resource and profile requirements, refusal sets, and safepoint\n   739\t   reconstruction obligations. Slot losslessness alone does not\n   740\t   establish execution parity, and version 3, like version 2, does not\n   741\t   translate between kernels.\n   742\t\n   743\tAnswers 7–9 are the Architect's (round 2) and are adopted:\n   744\t\n   745\t7. **Other profile tables.** Deferral is acceptable for a semantic-only\n   746\t   Draft; version 3 declares semantic support only and the other\n   747\t   profiles fail closed at the gate (§2) until their complete tables and\n   748\t   validation rules are published, with their profiles and code\n   749\t   addresses unchanged.\n   750\t8. **Legacy readers.** Direct version-aware lowering of versions 0–2,\n   751\t   preserving address and custody binding; normalization is a separate,\n   752\t   explicit, partial tool (§9) and a normalized checkpoint has a\n   753\t   different address that never silently replaces an admitted legacy\n   754\t   one.\n   755\t9. **Skeleton placeholder.** Safe only if the full value-union tag is\n   756\t   retained in the skeleton, which §6 now requires (`[:cell <class>]`,\n   757\t   never a bare class); and a placeholder does not canonicalize alias\n   758\t   topology, which is why §6 is now defined as the output of a\n   759\t   renaming-invariant refinement-and-search procedure.\n   760\t\n   761\t10. **Receiver work bound** (Architect, round 3; adopted as a\n   762\t    requirement, with the limits left to implementation and\n   763\t    composition). Before an implementation brief, the receiver must have\n   764\t    a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cells, traversal depth, refinement\n   765\t    work and search nodes for §6, with elapsed time as an optional\n   766\t    operational limit on top. Exceeding a local budget produces a\n   767\t    structured resource or capability refusal before any attachment, in\n   768\t    the qualified-unsupported family `datom.world.md` already provides;\n   769\t    it never labels a valid state `:noncanonical-cells`, never falls\n   770\t    back to encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check. The\n   771\t    exact limits and their refusal mapping are implementation and\n   772\t    composition decisions and must not alter canonical bytes.\n   773\t\n   774\t**Proposals, not consequences.** The following are design choices this\n   775\tdocument makes; they follow neither uniquely from the owner's three\n   776\trulings nor from the six invariants, and an implementation brief must\n   777\tname them as choices being accepted: a new body version (3) rather than\n   778\tanother carrier identification; the canonical representative of §6 as\n   779\tdefined by its procedure; the wire unit and pack framing of §3; the\n   780\texplicit fork-policy spelling of §5.5; semantic-only initial scope; the\n   781\tbyte-string and metadata restrictions of §4; SHA-256 as the row digest\n   782\t(§3); and positional rows over EAV (§1). Preserving the complete FFI\n   783\tenvelope (§5.3) is *not* on this list: it is the inherited behaviour,\n   784\tand narrowing it would have been the new protocol decision.\n   785\t\n   786\tStill open after this revision: none beyond the other-profile tables\n   787\t(§10.7) and the work-bound limits (§10.10).\n   788\t\n   789\t## 11. What this does not change\n   790\t\n   791\tCode identity, the contract stamp and profile registry, safepoints,\n   792\tpending-wait semantics and the r5 required keys, install-child rules,\n   793\tthe dependency closure and satisfaction check, the isolated store, the\n   794\tprivate resource table, module-store instancing, scheduler state, the\n   795\tcustody header and admission rules, fencing and operation ids, the\n   796\toutcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (two lift kinds and two\n   797\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added, §8). The carrier is a new body\n   798\tversion for the same facts. The\n   799\tArchitect's round-1 statement is the acceptance bar: tuple transport\n   800\tviolates none of the six invariants once polymorphic references and\n   801\tcontext edges are specified and validated, and §4, §3 and §8 are where\n   802\tthey are.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
