{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | sed -n '450,529p'; sed -n '308,340p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '710,724p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"   450\tpruning, and the representative it returns is canonical because the\n   451\tprocedure is deterministic and renaming-invariant, not because of any\n   452\tsymmetry argument.\n   453\t\n   454\t**The procedure.** Its input is the abstract task: the structure of\n   455\t§5 with cells as identities, before any numbering. Every encoding below\n   456\tis canonical CBOR (`dao.jing.cbor.md`) of a *synthetic vector\n   457\tstructure*; vectors are used throughout, never host maps or sets, so\n   458\tthat elements or keys that become equal after class substitution keep\n   459\ttheir multiplicity. All byte comparisons are bytewise on those CBOR\n   460\tbytes. Let the cells be `n` in number; if `n = 0` the procedure returns\n   461\tthe empty numbering and performs no search.\n   462\t\n   463\t1. **Class labels** are byte strings. A cell's initial label is the\n   464\t   CBOR of `[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`.\n   465\t2. **Class encoding** `CE(x)` of any encoded value or row, structural\n   466\t   and hash-free:\n   467\t   - `[:lit d]` → `[:lit d]`.\n   468\t   - `[:cell C]` → `[:cell <label of C>]` (full tag retained, §10.9).\n   469\t   - `[:row B]` → `[:row <CE(row B)>]`, the row expanded in place; the\n   470\t     expansion is finite because the structural edge family is acyclic\n   471\t     (§8 step 3).\n   472\t   - A row `[kind s₁ … sₖ]` → `[kind CE(s₁) … CE(sₖ)]` with each slot\n   473\t     encoded as: a structural datum (`A`, `M`, `P`, `S`, `N`, `D`, a\n   474\t     reason, a phase) as itself; a `V` as above; a cref as the expanded\n   475\t     row; a crefs vector as the vector of expansions.\n   476\t   - For `:set` rows, and for the key-value pairs of `:map`, `:store`\n   477\t     and `:bindings` rows and the entries of `:parked-table`, `:cells`\n   478\t     and the installs and module-stores slots: encode each element (or\n   479\t     each `[key value]` pair as a two-element vector), then **sort the\n   480\t     resulting vector of encodings bytewise, retaining duplicates**.\n   481\t     Ordered rows (`:vec`, `:list`, `:stack`, `:k`, frames) keep their\n   482\t     order.\n   483\t   - Context lookups (`P` in `:parked-ref` and `parked-id`, `M` in\n   484\t     `store-of`) are not expanded; they appear as the plain datum.\n   485\t   - An install child's `:task` row is **not expanded**: children are\n   486\t     numbered first, as their own tasks with their own cells (task\n   487\t     boundary), and a child cref appears in the parent's encodings as\n   488\t     `[:child <child root id>]`, the child's already-canonical root id.\n   489\t3. **Positions.** A position is the CBOR of the path from the task's\n   490\t   root to a cell reference, a vector of steps, each step one of\n   491\t   `[kind slot-index]` for a tabled slot, `[kind :elem CE(e)]` for an\n   492\t   element of a sorted row, `[kind :key CE(k)]` and `[kind :val CE(k)]`\n   493\t   for the key and value sides of a keyed row's pair, and `[:frame i]`\n   494\t   for the `i`th wait. Where several equal elements exist, equal\n   495\t   positions are repeated, one per occurrence.\n   496\t4. **Signature and refinement.** A cell's signature is the CBOR of\n   497\t   `[:sig <old label> <sorted vector of its positions, duplicates\n   498\t   retained>]`; its new label is that byte string. Repeat until no\n   499\t   class splits. Keeping the old label makes refinement monotone, so\n   500\t   the partition stabilizes in at most `n − k` splitting rounds from\n   501\t   `k` initial classes plus one stability check; this bounds partition\n   502\t   rounds only, not signature-processing or search work.\n   503\t5. **Search.** Order classes bytewise by label. If every class is a\n   504\t   singleton, assign `1 … n` in that order and stop. Otherwise take the\n   505\t   first non-singleton class and, **for each of its members in turn**,\n   506\t   give that member the label `[:ind <search depth> <old label>]`\n   507\t   (distinct from every refinement label by its tag; no ordering\n   508\t   relative to other labels is needed), refine to stability, and\n   509\t   recurse. Every branch is explored. Each complete leaf yields a\n   510\t   numbering; build the §5 rows under it and take the **root body's\n   511\t   canonical CBOR bytes** (the digest preimage, not the digest) as the\n   512\t   leaf's key. The procedure's output is the row set of the leaf with\n   513\t   the bytewise least key. Two leaves with equal keys have emitted\n   514\t   identical rows: they differ only in which source cell received\n   515\t   which number, which the transported representation cannot observe.\n   516\t\n   517\t**Validation.** Canonicality is a property of the emitted rows, not of\n   518\ta source-cell-to-number bijection, so the receiver does not compare\n   519\tnumberings. It decodes the rows to the abstract task, runs the\n   520\tprocedure, re-emits rows, and compares the resulting root id with the\n   521\tcarried root id: unequal is `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n   522\t`:noncanonical-cells`. Every labeling that yields the selected\n   523\tcommitment is thereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever\n   524\tdecides validity. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\n   525\tkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\n   526\tprocedure completes refuses with a structured resource refusal before\n   527\tany attachment and never labels the state `:noncanonical-cells`, never\n   528\tfalls back to encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check.\n   529\t\n\n### 5.3 Pending (content rows)\n\nOne kind, `:pending`, whose first slot is the reason and whose remaining\nslots are fixed per reason. The reasons are UCF §7.4.3's closed union as\nthe amendment r5 required-keys list fixes them; `:park` and\n`:call-effect` are not reasons and refuse as `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n| reason | slots after the reason |\n|---|---|\n| `:next` | `cell-C` |\n| `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id-D` |\n| `:ffi` | `cell-C  call-id-D  op` (op may be nil) |\n| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  envelope-V  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n| `:link-request` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C  envelope-D  op-id-D` |\n| `:link-response` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C` |\n| `:install` | `name-S` |\n\nThe retained FFI request's `envelope-V` is the complete envelope map,\nrecursively encoded under §4's ordinary rules: `[:lit m]` when it\nreaches no reference (the common case, arguments such as `[1 2]`), a\n`:map` row otherwise. Lower decodes it and retries it verbatim; it\nnever recomputes the arguments. Correlation must agree, not merely be present:\na cell's `:cell-body` stream is the pending's response stream; an\nenvelope's link id or call id equals the pending's; a `:link-request`\nenvelope names the pending's module. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\npresent exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\nwriter, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\nand subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, unique across the task and its children, absent\nwhen the body has no origin). `request-envelope` and `envelope` are the\nretained requests, retried verbatim. `call-id` and `link-id` are plain.\nAn `:install` pending is never sufficient alone: the root's installs\n        (unsatisfied!)\n        (let [envelope (:datom entry)]\n          {:yin.k/reason :ffi-request\n           :yin.k/call-id (entry-call-id (:engine (:v2 @found)) entry)\n           :yin.k/request-envelope (encode envelope)\n           :yin.k/request-op (or (apply2/request-op envelope)\n                                 (:op entry)\n                                 (:op (:k entry)))\n           :yin.k/request-args (apply2/request-args envelope)\n           :yin.k/request (marker-for! vm serve! found\n                                       (:stream-id entry))\n           :yin.k/response (marker-for! vm serve! found\n                                        (:stream-id route))\n           :yin.k/response-cell (cell-for! vm serve! found\n                                           (:id route))})))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-09 17:45:07 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-10 00:45:07 Asia/Bangkok\n\nReviewed `9d6c1cec9ae090eb227bf5bdf152516b00aac625`, the requested diff and sections, and the existing envelope handling. No files edited.\n\nThe envelope, gate, digest, and re-emission corrections pass. One substantive omission remains in the numbering grammar.\n\n**HIGH — canonicalization specification gap | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:472 | CE does not define bare cell identities.** It replaces `[:cell C]`, but pending rows carry bare `cell-C` and `response-cell-C` slots, and the `:cells` census has bare C keys. The structural-datum list deliberately enumerates other kinds but omits C. Thus CE is undefined for ordinary blocked reads and census entries. Treating these identities “as themselves” would instead leak incoming names into signatures and violate renaming invariance. | Define CE for **every identity-bearing position**, including pending slots and census keys, using the current class label rather than the incoming C. Specify whether census declaration occurrences contribute to reference-position multisets; either choice must be explicit and consistent.\n\n**MEDIUM — byte-contract gap | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:489 | Path construction still allows more than one spelling.** The grammar lists permissible steps but does not specify precisely when a sorted-row element step replaces an ordinary slot-index step, or how keyed-row pair steps compose with enclosing slot steps. Different implementations could produce different position bytes while following the listed vocabulary. | Define path construction per slot category: fixed slots, ordered repeated entries, unordered elements, keyed pairs, waits, and task boundaries. Unordered-entry paths must never include source enumeration indices. Pin search depth’s initial value and specify individualization labels explicitly as CBOR byte strings.\n\nProperties that passed:\n\n- Complete `envelope-V` preserves ordinary arguments, unknown keys, and recursively encoded guest values. It matches the existing handoff behavior, subject to the declared carrier refusals.\n- The active-path gate now permits shared children consistently with §3. Separate native restoration per install entry remains correct.\n- Explicit SHA-256 row hashing is consistent throughout; code addresses retain their governing function. The digest choice is correctly labelled a proposal.\n- Synthetic sorted vectors retain multiplicity after class substitution; full value-union tags preserve literal/reference distinctions.\n- The monotone partition bound and exhaustive search claims no longer overstate refinement.\n- Root **body** bytes are now an unambiguous leaf key. Equal keys imply equal complete commitments and emitted rows under the digest assumption.\n- Re-emission/root-id comparison correctly accepts equivalent automorphic labelings. It is consistent with §9’s fresh-receiver round-trip context once the procedure’s remaining inputs are fixed.\n- Numbering children first and treating their canonical root ids as opaque parent inputs respects task-local cells. This must run bottom-up during step 6, including when the parent has zero cells; it requires no native restoration.\n- Validation establishes reference completeness and acyclicity before recursive checks, preserves contextual checks, and delays attachment/restoration until validation succeeds.\n- The proposal list and the six-invariant architectural boundary remain sound.\n\nThe structure of step 12 is now appropriate. A universal safe-lowering claim still depends on a reproducible canonicality check, so findings above must be resolved first. I found no further architectural conflict in the requested corrections.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED — define bare-C class encoding and exact path construction, then recheck the resulting canonical byte procedure; merge publishes a Draft."}}
